<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Yiqu</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 272 BCE (approx.) · capital Yiqu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful Rong kingdom of the Loess Plateau that fought Qin for centuries, until a Qin queen dowager lured its king to his death in 272 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Yiqu were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong people</a> of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qingyang">Qingyang</a>. By the 5th century BCE they had built walled towns and formed a kingdom strong enough to defeat the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> in battle.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Yiqu combined farming with herding and controlled the uplands north of the Wei valley. Little is known of their institutions beyond Chinese accounts of wars and treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>. Excavated tombs of the region show a mixture of steppe-style ornaments and Chinese bronzes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a>, Queen Dowager Xuan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> had a liaison with the king of Yiqu and bore him two sons, then had him killed at the Ganquan palace in 272 BCE. Qin annexed his lands, created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longxi-commandery">Longxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beidi-commandery">Beidi</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> and built a long wall to protect them.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yiqu.jpg" alt="Yiqu" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:States_of_Zhou_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 272 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yiqu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yiqu Rong</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-536&amp;focus=yiqu" aria-label="Yiqu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7377 980 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yiqu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7697 1236h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7697 1236h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=-536&amp;focus=yiqu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

